The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (hn && remove_pending(hn, req)) {
    goto out_true;
}
if (test_and_set_bit(HANDSHAKE_F_REQ_COMPLETED, &req->hr_flags)) {
    ... return false;
}
out_true:
    sock_put(sk);
    return true;

Reason:
When a handshake request is cancelled for the first time, `remove_pending` returns true and the code jumps to `out_true` without setting the `HANDSHAKE_F_REQ_COMPLETED` flag. If a second cancellation occurs, `remove_pending` returns false (the request is no longer pending), so the code checks `test_and_set_bit`. Since `HANDSHAKE_F_REQ_COMPLETED` is not set, `test_and_set_bit` sets it and returns false, causing the condition to be false, and the code falls through to `out_true` again, performing another `sock_put(sk)` which leads to a refcount underflow.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (hn && remove_pending(hn, req)) {
    if (test_and_set_bit(HANDSHAKE_F_REQ_COMPLETED, &req->hr_flags)) {
        trace_handshake_cancel_busy(net, req, sk);
        return false;
    }
    goto out_true;
}

Reason:
The added `test_and_set_bit` in the first branch marks the request as completed immediately upon the first cancellation. If a duplicate cancellation arrives and `remove_pending` returns true (race condition) or false, the flag will already be set, causing the condition to be true and returning false before reaching `sock_put`. This prevents the double socket reference decrement and the refcount underflow.

